      "documentConcept": "GBA1, ambroxol and lysosomal-targeted therapies",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:cc458689f4ddeccea1e9f5808b6bc969df0e17b8ec99f4b3d68a7790cf194a8e",
      "text": "GBA1 variants are among the most common genetic risk factors for Parkinson's disease, so drugs aimed at the GCase lysosomal pathway have been tested as possible disease-modifying treatments. An open-label phase 2 ambroxol trial reported CSF penetration. Three later randomised trials reported no benefit on their primary outcomes: venglustat (MOVES-PD), ambroxol in Parkinson disease dementia, and Bial's BIA 28-6156 (ACTIVATE), which Bial then discontinued. The phase 3 ambroxol trial ASPro-PD was initiated in 2025. The registry lists the CAP-003 gene therapy trial as withdrawn. This is a research summary, not clinical advice.",
